Shoals.Stochastic: the time horizon must be non-negative; sqrt of a negative horizon is NaN, so every path value would be NaN
Assumption: a time horizon is a precondition of this sampler, not of its jump
count. Every sampler in Shoals.Stochastic forms `sigma * sqrt(t)` and a log
drift proportional to t, so `t < 0` makes `sqrt(t)` NaN and every path value
NaN -- a wrong answer dressed as an answer, which is the failure mode this
repository files bugs about. checked_horizon refuses instead.

Why the jump-count guards could not cover this (shoals#139): PR #131's
`gte(rate, zero)` clause keys on the PRODUCT `lambda * t`, and `0.0 * -1.0` is
`-0.0`, whose sign bit is set but which compares `>= 0.0` as true in IEEE 754.
At lambda = 0 there is no Poisson law to be negative, so that guard
legitimately admits the input and the NaN arrives from the diffusion side.
Guarding `t` itself rather than a product containing it is what closes it.

What this fixture does NOT assert: it does not pin the `-0.0` case. `t = -0.0`
stays ADMITTED on purpose -- `gte(-0.0, 0.0)` is true, `sqrt(-0.0)` is `-0.0`,
and the measured terminal values are s0 to within the log/exp round trip. The
positive-parity case for that lives in tests/stochastic.ch.

Deleting the checked_horizon call from THIS sampler does not make the fixture
pass: the assertion is one that NaN also fails, so the file would then fail on
the assertion rather than on the refusal, `--expect neg` would see the wrong
text, and it would report WRONG-DIAGNOSTIC. Deleting the guard from a DIFFERENT
sampler leaves this fixture green, which is why there is one file per sampler
rather than one file for the module.

This sampler had NO horizon validation at all before shoals#139 -- `sqrt(t)`
went straight through with nothing to bypass -- so this fixture pins a guard
that is new rather than one that was repaired. Measured on shoals@bba1a22 at
n = 8, seed 7: every path value came back NaN.

This entry point takes the per-step `dt` rather than a whole horizon, and it
is exported, so a caller reaches it without going through the two
heston_qe_*_terminal functions that validate t. It is checked for that reason.
